@startuml shared_dependency_shutdown

title Integration test: shared_dependency_shutdown\nA shared dependency must outlive the graceful shutdown of every dependent

participant "control_client_test_driver" as Driver
participant "Launch Manager" as LM

participant "component_shared\n(component_shared_process)" as Shared

box "dependent_process instances" #F0F4FA
participant "component_fast\n--stop-delay-ms 300" as Fast
participant "component_slow\n--stop-delay-ms 1500" as Slow
end box

database "POSIX shm counters\nstart / stop / violation" as Shm

' --- startup -----------------------------------------------------------------
activate LM
LM -> Driver ++: start (Run Target "Startup")
Driver -> LM: report_running()
LM --> Driver: callback: "Startup" activated

Driver -> LM: activate_run_target("run_target_diamond")

LM -> Shared ++: start
Shared -> Shm: ++start counter
Shared -> LM: report_running()

note over LM
  component_fast and component_slow only become ready once
  component_shared is active, so they start afterwards —
  and in parallel with each other.
end note

par
  LM -> Fast ++: start
  Fast -> Shm: ++start counter
  Fast -> LM: report_running()
else
  LM -> Slow ++: start
  Slow -> Shm: ++start counter
  Slow -> LM: report_running()
end

LM --> Driver: callback: "run_target_diamond" activated
Driver -> Shm: read start counter
Shm --> Driver: 3
Driver -> Shm: read stop counter
Shm --> Driver: 0

' --- shutdown ----------------------------------------------------------------
Driver -> LM: activate_run_target("Startup")

note over LM
  Stopping phase. component_fast and component_slow are not needed by
  "Startup", have no remaining dependents, and are therefore both ready
  to be deactivated at once.
end note

par
  LM -> Fast: SIGTERM
  Fast -> Fast: graceful shutdown work (300 ms)
else
  LM -> Slow: SIGTERM
  Slow -> Slow: graceful shutdown work (1500 ms)
end

Fast -> Shm: ++stop counter (now 1)
Fast --> LM: process exited
destroy Fast

note over LM
  component_fast is gone, but component_slow is still inside its
  graceful shutdown — it has been signalled, not stopped.
end note

alt #Pink current behaviour — the defect this test reproduces

  note over LM
    IComponent::active() already returns false for component_slow, because
    ProcessInfoNode::deactivate() clears reached_ready_ before it blocks on
    the shutdown wait. Transition::stopped() is !active(), so component_slow
    counts as stopped and allDependentsStopped(component_shared) is true.
  end note

  LM -> Shared: SIGTERM  (too early)
  Shared -> Shm: read stop counter
  Shm --> Shared: 1
  Shared -> Shm: ++violation counter (expected 2, saw 1)
  Shared -> Shm: ++stop counter (now 2)
  Shared --> LM: process exited
  destroy Shared

  Slow -> Shm: ++stop counter (now 3)
  Slow --> LM: process exited
  destroy Slow

  note over Shm
    The stop counter still ends at 3, so counting stops alone does not
    catch the bug. The violation counter is what discriminates.
  end note

else #F2FAF2 required behaviour — what the test asserts

  Slow -> Shm: ++stop counter (now 2)
  Slow --> LM: process exited
  destroy Slow

  note over LM
    Only now is every dependent of component_shared actually gone.
  end note

  LM -> Shared: SIGTERM
  Shared -> Shm: read stop counter
  Shm --> Shared: 2
  note right of Shared: expectation met — no violation recorded
  Shared -> Shm: ++stop counter (now 3)
  Shared --> LM: process exited
  destroy Shared

end

' --- verdict -----------------------------------------------------------------
LM --> Driver: callback: "Startup" activated

Driver -> Shm: read violation counter
Shm --> Driver: 0 required / 1 observed
note right of Driver
  EXPECT_EQ(violation_counter.get(), 0)
  <b>fails today</b> with 1.
end note

Driver -> Shm: read stop counter
Shm --> Driver: 3
deactivate Driver
deactivate LM

legend bottom
  The two dependents are stopped in parallel and both keep running after
  SIGTERM, which is what makes the race observable: the fast one finishes
  while the slow one is still shutting down.

  Timings are chosen so the window is deterministic — 300 ms vs 1500 ms,
  both well inside the configured shutdown_timeout_ms of 5000 ms, so no
  SIGKILL escalation interferes.
end legend

@enduml
